A||N||5||FieroJoe||Good way to paint ext. trim items||||6||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||03-14-2001||10:54 PM||||Through much experimentation with different methods and types of spray paint for my exterior trim, I think I have found what provides a good shine and (I hope) good durability.<P>I have removed most of the trim on my 87 coupe to repaint them.  Most all of the moldings, marker lights, engine vents (also removed stamped louver sheet), 1/4 windows and other small items.<P>The procedure I used:<P>sanding and filling in all the imperfections<P>Priming with Ace Rust-Stop spray primer (2 coats)<P>Sanding again.<P>Spraying Ace Rust-Stop Satin Black paint for base coat (about 3-4 light coats)<P>Waiting 5 days for full hardness.<P>Spraying about 5-7 light coats of Ace Spray Gloss Polyurethane.<P>Results are good, and I think the polyurethane will help to keep the shine longer.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/trimrepaint1.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||6
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||03-14-2001||11:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B><P>Results are good, and I think the polyurethane will help to keep the shine longer.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do you use gloss on all your trim???<BR>  I use the NAPA semi-gloss trim paint...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||85seNUT||03-15-2001||01:13 AM||||how the heck did u get all that stuff off.  is it just screws?||||reg||1
Z||000003||85seNUT||03-15-2001||01:15 AM||||how the heck did u get all that stuff off.  is it just screws?||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||03-15-2001||10:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>how the heck did u get all that stuff off. is it just screws?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The marker lights are just 2 screws each.  The long pieces of molding are held on by clips.  You can break the clips out if you are careful, but the best way that I have found is to take off the inner door panel and push the little tabs out from the inside.  That way you may be able to salvage the clips.<P>The other shorter ones use a different type clip, and I think I remember just pulling them out carefully.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Do you use gloss on all your trim?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes I did.  I know it does not look stock but I like it.  Top coat on all of my exterior trim is high gloss polyurethane.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||03-15-2001||11:58 AM||||Another thing that works really well on the exterior black plastic trim pieces is flexible black bumper paint in the spray can. It's durable as hell and it has a nice satin finish. I've used it to paint my bumper pads and side trim and it looks original.||||reg||1
